WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.550
When we talk about the future of work

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.610
and we talk about what would happen

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.613
if we don't move on diversity,
equity and inclusion,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.680
I don't honestly think
that there is a future,

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.120
that that's possible.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.250
Studies have shown that more diverse teams

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.470
tend to perform better.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.160
And many companies have made commitments

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.620
to hiring a more diverse workforce.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.440
Yet creating inclusive work cultures

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.940
that are truly equitable and free of bias

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.440
remains a formidable
challenge for companies,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.800
especially since some
of the biggest obstacles

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.323
are the very employees
they're trying to change.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.380
One of the challenges in this space

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.270
is that people do have
really strong feelings

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.540
about what they should
do and what's right,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.730
but the strategies that
organizations have used,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.790
pretty much universally,
are not effective.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:48.660
They're ineffective and
sometimes make things worse.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.190
So many of us have incredible intentions,

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.310
but terrible actions.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.770
And unless you can get your
intention and your action

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.920
to align, then your intention is in vain.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.110
But there may be actions

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.520
that companies and
their employees can take

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.464
that can mean change is
finally starting to happen.

00:01:05.464 --> 00:01:08.110
If business would step in

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.830
and do this work, I think
it would change society

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.920
outside of the walls of business.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.220
And yes, it is disruptive.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.370
Technology was disruptive.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.730
Think about the companies that said,

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.280
we don't really wanna touch technology.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.610
They're not here anymore.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.181
But from the disruption comes change.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:30.343
So, there's

00:01:30.343 --> 00:01:32.750
a very strong link between companies

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.510
who are strong at diversity,
equity and inclusion

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.410
and increased organizational performance.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.010
This is Dr. David Rock.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.700
He's the CEO and Co-Founder

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.780
of the NeuroLeadership Institute,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.450
a consultancy company
that advises organizations

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.670
on management strategies based on science

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.650
and evidence-based practices.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.710
The most kind of salient way
to understand this is that

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.990
we all see the world
very, very differently

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:00.040
and when you get a homogeneous team,

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.890
or a team, that's all basically the same,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:02.970
they actually make more

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.840
cognitive errors.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:05.820
They don't see errors

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.300
that other people have made

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.030
and they're far less creative
and far less innovative

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.550
than a diverse team.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.490
While these diverse groups

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:16.710
may be more efficient,
creative and faster at finding

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.090
and solving problems,

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.270
they often feel like

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:20.630
they're being less efficient

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.967
and less confident in their work,

00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:24.170
partly due to a phenomenon

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.060
called cognitive elaboration.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.030
Just a long word for saying,

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.250
you're gonna think harder.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.790
The teams are thinking harder.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.990
Thinking is generally aversive

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:37.640
and so we feel more confident
and comfortable in teams

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.990
that are more homogeneous

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.800
where people basically agree with us

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.820
all the time.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.790
And it turns out, that feels good,

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.010
but the team is nowhere
near as successful.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.110
If companies want to
see the positive effects

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:48.943
of a diverse

00:02:48.943 --> 00:02:51.760
and inclusive workforce, they
need to find ways to mitigate

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.800
that naturally occurring friction.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.400
Essentially they need to have

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.416
their employees be comfortable
being uncomfortable.

00:02:58.416 --> 00:03:00.850
In business, we go to work,

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.820
we do our jobs and we leave.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.920
And we are so weary

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.990
of pushing against the grain

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.410
with a colleague, with a
coworker, with an employer,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:11.990
et cetera, with an employee.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.170
And I think if we truly want to grow

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.940
and get to know each other better,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.570
we do have to embrace some of
that uncomfortable dialogue

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.070
for the sake of growth.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.850
This is former NFL linebacker

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.590
and Fox Sports Analyst, Emmanuel Acho.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.350
And in 2020, he launched a YouTube series

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.380
all about getting uncomfortable.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.100
Welcome to another episode

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.950
of Uncomfortable Conversations
with a Black Man.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.580
When was the last time you sat down

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.470
just to have a conversation

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.703
with a group of Black people, Garret?

00:03:43.014 --> 00:03:43.847
I could be honest with you.

00:03:43.847 --> 00:03:45.050
I don't know that I've
ever had a conversation

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.660
with a group of Black people.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.490
I created Uncomfortable
Conversations with a Black Man

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.350
out of an overflow of my heart.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.240
After the murder of George Floyd,

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.510
I realized I had to do something.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:56.600
The series would go on

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.400
to amass over 17 million
views, inspire two books

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.070
and most recently,
Emmanuel launched a podcast

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:07.420
with Accenture North America
CEO, Jimmy Etheredge.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.010
There's so many CEO,
executives at companies,

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.370
they want to serve those under
them, or those with them.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.200
But they think they're doing what's best,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.450
but unless you ask them what is best,

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:20.210
you will be sitting here
spinning your wheels

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:22.275
and actually not serving
the person at all.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:29.190
Primary example, Jimmy and
I recently spoke to a woman

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.220
who was hearing disabled and
we started that conversation

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.900
on that episode.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.690
I used words like,

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.010
that she was different,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.350
you know, modified and all

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:41.740
these other fanciful words.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.200
But the first question I asked,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.123
hey, I'm gonna start with this,

00:04:45.123 --> 00:04:47.660
disabled, is that wrong to say

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:48.493
or not?

00:04:48.493 --> 00:04:51.640
And she actually told me she
cringed when I said different.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.892
She said, because she's not
different, she is disabled

00:04:54.892 --> 00:04:56.570
and she doesn't find any weakness in that,

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.640
but she actually finds strength in that.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.730
If I don't start with

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.000
what is they very uncomfortable question,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.640
I'm sitting here thinking I'm being polite

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.510
to try to, you know, be
kind and in all honesty

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.390
she's cringing on the inside.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.677
And that's what so many
of us do in the workplace.

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:13.017
Well, this is what I'm
gonna do to make sure

00:05:13.017 --> 00:05:14.477
I help these Black men out.

00:05:14.477 --> 00:05:16.810
This is what I'm gonna do to
help out these Black women.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.830
And so, my advice is, talk to the people

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:21.960
in whom you are trying to serve.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.540
Talk to the people in whom
you are trying to honor.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.410
I had great intentions.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.780
My intentions were amazing
by trying to use a word

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.430
that I perceived to be
more kind than disabled.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.200
My actions, terrible.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.860
In the last decade or so,

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.220
more businesses have promised

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.330
to make diversity a major priority.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.400
Tech giants like, Apple,
Google and Facebook

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.110
have committed to sharing their
internal diversity numbers

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.410
and annual reports.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:48.740
And while there has been an increase

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.050
in the overall percentage
of employees of color

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.230
at these companies, for some groups,

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.690
only minimal progress has been made.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:58.420
When it comes to Black
or Latinx employees,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.450
their percentage in the last six years

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.143
has only been raised to 1-3%.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:05.100
While hiring is only step one,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.500
retaining and growing diversity
is a major focus as well

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:11.130
and something that companies
have also struggled with.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.030
Just having bodies in the building,

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:14.530
doesn't get you anywhere.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:16.740
You have to be able to
leverage that diversity.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.430
And that means you need
systems, you need metrics,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:20.820
you need accountability.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.380
This is Janet Stovall,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.730
a veteran of the corporate
communications world

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:26.370
and a Senior Client Strategist

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.550
for the NeuroLeadership Institute.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:32.630
At NLI, we talk about
priorities, habits and systems.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.430
So, the first thing you have to do,

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.400
is you have to make this a priority

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.060
and it has to be a priority
like any other business priority

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.670
that a company decides
that it wants to pursue.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:45.720
Then you have to look at the habits

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:49.410
that are in place to make people either

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.330
live up to those priorities, or not.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.440
And so, once you prioritize,
you look at the habits

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:55.810
that are getting in the
way of that priority

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:56.730
and then you build a system

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.860
to make sure that you
can change those habits.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.900
But according to NLI research,

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.850
some of the most common
strategies that companies turn to

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:05.730
in order to create inclusion,

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.820
can often have the opposite effect.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.045
One example is in the area
of unconscious bias training.

00:07:12.045 --> 00:07:13.750
It feels really

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:17.070
intuitive to mandate,
you know, bias education.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.070
You know, bias is clearly a problem.

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.120
It seems perfectly logical to say,

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.900
well, look, everyone should
really go through bias training.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.570
Now, there's very good evidence
that that does nothing,

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:27.490
or makes things worse.

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:28.940
I mean, you might get a program

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:31.740
that gets a small amount
of results, but on average

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.300
requiring people to do
awareness-building bias training,

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.670
is not gonna help very much.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.270
In 2016, researchers who studied

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:43.240
30 years of data from over 800 companies

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.330
wrote in the, Harvard Business Review,

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.810
that the positive effects
of diversity training

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.960
rarely lasts beyond a day or two

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.010
and a number of studies suggest
that it can activate bias,

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:54.870
or spark a backlash.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:58.240
Now, bias is everywhere
'cause at the end of the day

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.230
everybody is born with bias.

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:04.770
The problem is when bias
gets into business decisions,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.600
we sometimes make sub-optimal decisions.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.320
And we actually can't see

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.820
the vast majority of our own biases,

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:14.010
no matter how much we want to,

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.420
or how much we know about them.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:16.660
But what we realized is

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:20.330
you could do something
about bias in teams.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.900
Their strategy takes
over 150 forms of bias

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.000
and groups them into
five broad categories.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.850
Then, instead of asking individuals

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:30.890
to self-police their own actions,

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:33.450
companies can create
systems to encourage teams

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:37.900
to get into the habit of
identifying biases as they happen.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:38.733
What we found is

00:08:38.733 --> 00:08:41.620
when you teach that to
people relatively quickly,

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.770
people start to see these
biases as they happen

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:49.770
and relatively quickly,
they're able to label bias

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.600
in a process, or in a decision

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.200
and then work out what
to do to address it.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.740
And our studies are ongoing,
of course, in this space,

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.420
but we're seeing about 78% of people

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.950
who learn this framework,
start to use it weekly.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.920
At least one to three times a week.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:08.180
It doesn't get us anywhere
pointing the finger.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.790
What we have to be able to do

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:11.570
instead of giving people labels,

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.210
is we have to be able to label behaviors.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:15.800
If we can label behaviors

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.860
and then agree on a common
language for those behaviors,

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.900
we can talk about what people do

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:22.630
separate from what they are.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:25.540
So, labeling people racist doesn't help

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:26.540
in the business workplace.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:27.810
It just doesn't help at all.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.100
But coming up with things that
help you change the behavior

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.656
that might be racist behavior,
that's what changes things.

00:09:37.376 --> 00:09:39.130
One of the most important things to do

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.040
with each and every individual

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.050
is what we call, find common ground.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:48.290
And this is a habit of
creating in-group with people

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:51.640
and really bringing each
of you into the same group,

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:53.100
in a sense.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.630
Or in the brain, it's both
of you being in this circle

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:58.060
that we all draw around ourselves

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.250
and being on that same team.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:02.540
And so, the habit is in every interaction,

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.380
whether you're an employee,
or a manager, or a leader.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.340
In every interaction,

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.810
you're really finding common
ground with that person,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.280
ideally creating shared goals.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.350
And this creates a really
strong sense of inclusion.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.990
I heard it said one time, you
know, I can't change hearts,

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:19.550
but I can change laws.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:20.950
Well, I'd like to paraphrase that and say,

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.260
you know, I can't change what you think,

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.350
but I can certainly change what you do.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.200
And as long as I change what you do,

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.270
I'll end up getting the
results that I want.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:31.440
Improving workplace
diversity and inclusion,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:33.630
isn't just about building
one set of habits,

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:36.100
or eliminating a certain behavior

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.040
and it is likely to be uncomfortable.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:41.140
But if the future of
work can truly leverage

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.060
diverse and inclusive cultures,

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:46.203
it can have effects beyond
just the company's bottom line.

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:51.010
I truly believe that
the business workplace

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:53.660
is the only place that some
people ever come into contact

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.420
with anybody who's different from them.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.380
We can spend our lives around people

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:00.530
who are no different from us
outside of work all we want,

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.830
but if we go to work and
if you have an organization

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.720
that decides it is committed

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.640
to diversity, equity and inclusion

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:12.420
and it is committed enough that
it is gonna have priorities,

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:16.850
habits and systems in
place to make that grow

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:20.200
and be what it needs to
be, you have a choice.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:21.400
If you work in that environment,

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.440
you're either gonna get
on with it, or you're not.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.800
And so, I think that the
business is the one entity

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.420
that could actually change this

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:31.710
'cause no place else has that reach,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.220
no place else has that context,

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:37.530
no place else has the power,
the money and the resources

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.160
to change things than
the commercial world.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.330
And so, if we step into
this and do this work,

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:43.780
I think it'll change society.

